had a discretionary fund. So, he could just put something

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.200
in development without having to go to anyone else. And

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.200
we got a call saying, 'we'd like to turn this play into a

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:57.100
movie and George Clooney, and Leo DiCaprio want to produce.'

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.700
Remember, I'd never had a production of a play. I

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:10.000
was temping at a place in Rockefeller Plaza where my job

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:17.200
was to staple things and put them in the mail. And I said,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.800
let me think about it. I'll get back to you next week.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.200
No, I sh[__] myself. And then, when I cleaned that up, 

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.000
I went out to LA and I met with George's

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.700
people and eventually George and Leo, nd the Warner

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:45.100
Brothers' folks, and it was happening, you know, so rewiring

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.800
brain to that, which was totally new for me, was hard,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.400
but also thrilling. And I did a few drafts and then I turned

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.500
it over to George Clooney and Grant Heslov, and they worked

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.700
off those drafts and did their drafts and at the point

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.600
I handed it off, my work was done. It was very much at

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.400
that point going to be George's film. I mean, he was directing

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.900
it. He was in it. He had pulled together the cast, which

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.700
was an amazing cast, and he had found the financing and

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.600
he had wrested it away from Warner Brothers to get it distributed

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.400
by Sony, which never - it's very difficult to wrest

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.800
a property away from one studio for it to go to another. So,

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.700
it's very much his thing and and they did their work and

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.000
they changed a lot, and a lot of the screenplay for

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.500
that film is them and I was fine with that because

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.000
I knew they understood the core of the story and they didn't

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.400
want to mess with that. And that was this young man, at

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:47.400
this fork in the road, having this great sort of ethical

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.800
choice before him and he chooses one direction and another.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.800
And in the moment, he chooses this direction, as the moment.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.800
He becomes a monster and it's that moment of becoming a

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.900
monster which I wanted to arrive at and that is what they

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.300
achieved, I think in the film, in a way that was very different

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.400
from the play. And they got beautiful performances out

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.800
of everyone and I'm very proud of the work

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.900
that we all did, but particularly grateful to George for

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.400
being so passionate about it, making it happen because

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.200
those sort of films. I mean, look around, they're not

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.800
getting made anymore. Certainly, well, at least not in

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.600
America, as much. If you don't have a comic book hero, good

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.600
luck, you know, and so I mean all in all for me, it

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.900
was incredibly positive experience and it opened up. It

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.300
opened up that door that I got my pinky toe into and

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.600
you know, there's no doubt in my mind that I wouldn't

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:53.800
be sitting here if it wasn't for a number of people, Kevin

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.000
McCormick, the junior exec that worked for him. George Clooney,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.200
Grant Heslov, all the actors that said yes to that film,

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.000
the people that put the money into it. If it weren't for

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.100
all of those people taking a chance on this piece of material

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.400
by someone who had no credits whatsoever, then

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.500
I'd still be stapling stuff and mailing it. So. you have

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.600
Farragut North and you get the call about doing the movie,

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:20.100
but then you get BAFTA and Oscar nominated and

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.300
then a call about House of Cards. You've written for stage,

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:26.500
you've then written for the big screen. Was there any hesitancy

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.800
then by going, 'Well now  I've got to learn how to write for television.'

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.700
And is that much of a different experience? Yeah. Again,

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.900
it's a little messy because it's not that linear. The way

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.200
I'd gotten that agent which is how Ides of March happened

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:44.100
is by writing a pilot for AMC. AMC's the network that

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:50.000
did Mad Men and Breaking Bad and Walking Dead. But when

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.900
I got the gig, they were just starting and a good friend

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:56.600
of mine, Sam Foreman, had a really good meeting with

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:01.700
them. He had an agent. He had shows produced

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:05.400
on the stage and they had said to him, 'We love your spec

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.200
script. We're looking for three things, as we start this

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.500
new venture of original content. We're looking for period

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:18.200
pieces, espionage pieces or smart horror and do you have

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.500
any of those things?' And Sam said, 'I do not' and they said,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.900
come back when you do and he said 'okay' and then Sam and

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.800
I went for a drink and he said, 'do you have any ideas for

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.600
period espionage or smart horror?' And I said, 'what the

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.700
f[__] is smart horror?' He's like, 'I have no idea'. I guess

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.800
it's the Walking Dead, right? But we're like, okay,

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.200
we don't know what smart horror is. So, our best

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.700
bet here is espionage period and then there was a few

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.500
screenplays that I wrote. One was a blind script for

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.400
Warner Brothers that was part of the Ides of March deal.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:58.345
I hubristically try to tackle Tale of Two Cities. I was like

00:09:58.345 --> 00:10:01.700
the sixth writer and twenty years to try to make that happen

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:05.300
for Warner Brothers and I think I wrote a good script and

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.600
I think the five scripts before me were also really good.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.300
They just couldn't commit. And then I wrote a couple

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:16.100
other screenplays that didn't get made, and 

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:18.800
I was sort of at this moment where I go, 'well, I had this

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.800
movie that George Clooney made, and got nominated for

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.700
the stuff. And I got my foot in the door. And now, I've

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:28.300
had three scripts that haven't gotten made. It

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:32.400
may be ending.' I mean, you know that you can only write

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.000
so many scripts that don't get made before people stop

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.700
hiring you to write scripts and it was right at that moment

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:43.200
that Fincher called and said, 'have you heard of this thing called

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:47.200
House of Cards? They want to make a TV show'. I'd heard

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.300
of it because if you write anything about politics at some

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.900
point, someone's going to say, 'Did you watch House of Cards?'

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.700
And at that time they meant just the BBC version, ours didn't

00:10:55.700 --> 00:11:00.300
exist. So I've been meaning to watch it anyway, and I figured

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.500
well Fincher is one of the great living filmmakers and

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:07.300
within ten seconds you realize when you're talking

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:10.400
David Fincher, that you're dealing with the sort of mind

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.300
that you may never encounter again. I mean, he has a savate-

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:17.400
like knowledge of the craft in the history of filmmaking.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:26.200
He is incredibly clever, and witty, but also concise and

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:30.100
deep and blunt and profound in his thinking about art.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:35.000
His synapses fire faster than just about anyone 

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:40.300
I know and I just felt through the phone, like I was dealing

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:43.800
with this life force that I wanted to tap into. I wanted to

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.300
hold onto and work with, and be around and we started

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:52.000
talking about House of Cards. And we had similar

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.300
instincts on the approach to it, and we were starting to

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:58.700
come up with new ideas on the fly. And it just became clear

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:02.300
that I had to do this. We're going to open to questions

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:05.000
from the audience in a couple of minutes. I just wanted

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.500
to ask, I'll be quick. That's good. I mean I shouldn't

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:19.200
have prepared so much. I could have gone to the pub. Structure.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:22.200
so you have the structure of a play, you have the structure of a film.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.500
How does a structure differ when you're doing television

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.800
or does it differ that much given that it's not really TV,

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.900
everything's dropping the same

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:35.400
time. Yeah. Well for me TV's a lot

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.800
closer to the theatre because there's an ongoing dialogue

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:41.900
with the actors and the department heads.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.700
It's like you have this crew and this family that you're

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:47.900
spending a lot of time with, and just discovering things with

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:53.000
as you would in the rehearsal process for a play. And something

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.000
that you discover in say episode 2, even though you've

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.900
planned out the whole season, might influence scripts to come

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.300
and have this ripple effect. And so there's a lot more

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.300
dialogue between the collaborators going back and forth.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.500
Then I think you have on a film, where most the time you

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.500
have the script and then the director takes it and directs

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.800
it. There's also the fact that you have just one season

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.000
for us, thirteen hours. A film is much more like a short

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.700
story, or a poem, not a moment or line that can

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:31.100
be wasted. It has to resolve itself in 90 to 120

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:35.400
minutes. And with a television show, it doesn't. I mean,

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:39.900
each hour chapter, whatever you want to call it, is a discrete

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.800
thing that has its own beginning, middle, and an end to

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.700
a degree, but it doesn't have to completely resolve itself.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:49.500
I mean, it could actually end in a place of total lack

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.700
of resolution, but the feeling you're heading somewhere,

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.600
so you can really take your time with the storytelling,

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.800
I think dive into character in ways that are impossible

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:05.400
to do in film, or even a stage play. Because you have the

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.300
hours to do it. In that way, I think it's closest to a novel

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:15.000
more than anything else, which is also how people are watching

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
it to these days. They're sort of picking it up and putting

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.400
it down when they want to, and choosing how many

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:24.000
chapters they want to read. And you have no control over

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.100
that, the same with a novelist doesn't have any control

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:29.300
over, whether you read their novel on the train

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:31.900
or in your living room, in one sitting or over the course

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:36.700
of many months. So, you know, the approach that Fincher

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:41.000
wanted to take and I guess I didn't know

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.400
any better. I mean none of us have made TV. We didn't

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:47.200
really know what we're doing, but we just figured, okay,

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:51.700
we'll make a film that just happens to be thirteen hours long.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:58.500
And I can't describe to you what that means other than,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:02.300
that's what we tried to do. And I think that's what a season

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:08.900
is, for us. So, we don't really think about episodes as much

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:13.800
as we think about the film, and we just have a lot

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:17.700
longer film, and more time to play with than the typical film.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:23.000
So if we have a couple of roving mics, so if you have a

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:26.400
question, can you stick your hand in the air? Let's start

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:31.000
over here. In House of Cards season 1, and I think every

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:36.500
episode eight or nine, Francis offers Doug to teach him

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:42.300
how to play chess. My question is, would Doug have faired much better,

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:45.700
if you taken up that offer? or sort of more generally

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:49.300
speaking, how far is chess maybe an organizing principle

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:55.100
to the entire show? I believe when Francis offered

00:15:55.100 --> 00:16:01.300
to teach Stamper how to play chess, Claire had disappeared

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:05.100
off to New York to be with Adam Galloway, and he was feeling

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:10.300
sort of in this restless purgatory and Stamper, if anything

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:16.400
is functional, it's about control. It's about getting

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:21.030
things done in a very practical way and he wasn't, certainly

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:25.800
not in season one, equipped, to deal with the emotional

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:31.400
limbo that his boss was in. So better to just get away.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.500
I've never thought about it in terms of would he

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:38.200
have fared better, if he had learned how to play chess.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:44.300
I don't know. Maybe, I mean, chess is, and this bleeds into

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:48.200
your second question I guess, chess to me is

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.700
fascinating. I wrote a play about Garry Kasparov

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:58.100
match against Deep Blue in 1997. And I'm not

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:01.100
even an amateur chess player. I mean, I can hold my own

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:06.500
with the average player out there, but it's vast.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:11.100
I read that there were more possible

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.500
games of chess than there are particles in the universe

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:19.700
and when studying someone like a Garry Kasparov, it was

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.100
actually someone I brought up a lot my early conversations.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:25.800
with Fincher. What's interesting about Kasparov

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.200
in the reason he's one of the greatest players of all

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:32.400
time is not because of his strategy or tactics. It's not

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.400
that he's thinking 15 moves ahead. It's that he's creating

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:42.200
chaos on the board. He will sacrifice two pieces for seemingly 

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:47.000
no reason whatsoever, with not necessarily a concrete plan

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.900
on how to get back that capital. But he knows that the

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:53.100
person on the other side of the table's saying, 'why did he

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.600
just do that? He must have some grand plan. He must be

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.600
thinking 15 moves ahead'. And at that level with Grandmasters

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:03.800
it's about, it's like tennis, who makes the first mistake.

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:08.100
So if I know I'm willingly making this mistake, but what

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.000
I get back is I'm psychologically throwing off the other

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.400
guy who will make a bigger mistake than it, then it's worth

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:19.900
it. And that's something we we use all the time with Frank

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.100
Underwood. To steal one of the lines from

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.300
the show, 'if you don't like the way the table is set,

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:29.300
turn over the table' and something that Fincher and I talked

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:35.200
a lot about was that politics is it's not a

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:43.200
symphony. It's jazz. It is call and response. It is reaction.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:47.800
The great politicians might plan out 15 steps ahead, but

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:51.400
they'll get to step 2 and everything changes. There's some

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:54.900
crisis, there's some unexpected thing. There is the chaos of

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:58.400
the world. And at that moment, you have to get rid of the

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:02.900
next 13 steps and you have to react to that and develop

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:06.900
a new plan that will the next day probably be

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:10.000
thrown out the window because of new chaos introducing itself.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:13.200
And it's how do you manage that chaos and make it work for

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:17.600
you. How do you play jazz with it? That really makes the

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:23.900
great politicians great. And then the very best politicians

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:29.500
know how to create chaos and make it work for them. So

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:33.600
yeah, I mean, I guess in that respect. Yeah, I'm not thinking

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.300
about chess all the time as I write House of Cards, but

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:40.400
there's certainly plenty of parallels to be drawn. 

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:42.300
Does that metaphor hold true for the process of actually

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.200
writing the show as well? You may be thinking

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.900
oh this is going to happen in episode 13'. Oh, yeah all the time.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:52.900
Totally. I mean from from creative discoveries

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:56.400
to things that are simply out of our control, creative

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.800
discoveries might be something like in season 1. Peter

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:02.100
Russo's character was a big character, but never intended

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.400
to be the guy who ran for governor, that was a whole other

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.800
character, but Corey Stoll was so great, and when we saw

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.800
him on screen with Kevin, we all felt just, we want to see

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:15.100
more of that. I mean that's lightning in a bottle that

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.700
we've caught that you don't want to let that lightning go.

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:22.500
So I made a huge shift in the middle of filming the season

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.100
where I took this other character story who was running

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:28.000
for governor, and I gave it to Peter Russo. Now, that's

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:31.400
not just about changing whose name is above the dialogue.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:35.100
That required page one rewrites from episode five onwards,

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:39.500
but it was in response to this discovery we had made and

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.300
it was totally worth it because It actually made Peter

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:47.500
Russo story stronger. It consolidated the story for the

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.800
season. So, we were really focusing on this one guy as opposed

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:56.100
to two. And you know, it worked for us. There are other

00:20:56.100 --> 00:21:03.000
instances where you know, you might lose an actor. I mean,

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:07.600
in the States, you can have series regular deals, and forgive

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:10.990
me for being a little jargony here, but a series regular deal

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:15.465
means that basically you own the actor. You have them

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:16.500
in first position. They can't do anything else unless they

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.300
get permission from you. But there's a lot of characters

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:21.800
that you don't have in first position because you can't

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:24.900
afford it. So they might be in a fair number of

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:27.700
episodes and you kind of count on their existence, but

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:30.100
they get some big offer from another thing and boom, they're

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:33.700
gone, and then you built the story around this person that

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:39.100
is no longer available to you. You have no choice but to

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:42.200
change your story and react to it. So I'm not going to

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:45.400
name names, but I mean, that is happened before for us.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:51.400
And, you know, look here's a tiny one, it wasn't global but,

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:56.800
you know, you guys remember in season 1, episode 9, I think

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.800
where Francis says 'I despise children. There. I said

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:05.500
it' and in the beginning of that episode, he's talking about

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.400
stuff with Peter Russo. Russo's kids are running around.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:13.400
One of Russo's kids knocks some coffee. It burns his hand and

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:16.400
he said, 'I despise children. There. I said it' and then the

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:21.500
rest of the episode you see him wearing a bandage. Well,

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:25.200
Kevin had actually injured his hand and there was this

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:28.100
bandage there and we didn't want to deal with having to

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:31.400
shoot everything where we are hiding this hand, so I created

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:34.900
that opening to the show so that we could justify him wearing

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:37.600
a bandage. But then I'm like, well, I might as well make

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:40.500
the most of it. Let's have him say he despises children.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:43.800
Yeah. It's one of my favorite direct addresses, but that's

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.000
just the chaos of the world, you know.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.300
Firstly about the Ides of March, which is a film I love

00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:52.900
very much and fortunately saw it in New York and it was

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:57.300
a really fantastic experience for us when it came out. You

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:01.700
touched slightly on your research, which obviously

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:05.800
for that came from your experience. So firstly, what tools

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:11.200
do you use for researching for other things, other

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:14.700
projects? And the other aspect is about the male and the

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:20.100
female voice within your work as a writer. So, within me,

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:22.900
Ides of March, even though it was quite a male heavy film,

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:27.100
you had very strong female voices, and this is also existent

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:30.900
within House of Cards. How do you as a male writer write

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:36.400
so well for women? Well, thank you for liking Ides of March

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:39.600
and for the compliment you paid me at the end, writing

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:43.200
well for women, I mean, I'll start with the second part

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:46.100
first, which is that it's not just me writing well for

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.700
women, it's also the women I employ on my writing staff,

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:52.000
you know, which is an important thing to do. If you're

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.400
going to have female characters in your show that said

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:59.300
I don't, I really am against the notion that the female

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:01.910
writers should write the female characters and the male writers

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:05.700
should write the male characters. I mean, what is writing,

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:09.600
if not the attempt to put yourself in other people's shoes?

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:13.500
so, I expect my female writers to be able to write men

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:18.100
well to the best of their ability and vice versa. Now,

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:22.400
we are all limited by our own experiences. There are certain

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:26.700
things, I will never be able to access because of things

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.500
that are genetic, things that are in my upbringing, things

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:35.800
that are cultural. We all have our limited horizons and

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:41.400
often our unconscious biases, but I think writing is an

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:47.200
attempt to acknowledge those and sometimes get beyond

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:50.500
them and find the universal in all of our experiences.

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:55.900
So, the way we approach writing men and women on House

00:24:55.900 --> 00:25:01.500
of Cards: it's paradoxical; On the one hand, we want to

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:06.500
be gender blind. I mean, all the characters

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:11.400
are equally intelligent and venal. Balzac is

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.000
one of my favorite writers because the women in his stories

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:18.200
are just as insidious as everyone else, and I think that

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:22.200
we're looking for the strength and also the weakness

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:24.900
in every character and there's a term that

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.400
is, I don't know if it's used here often, but certainly

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:30.200
in the States, like 'strong female characters'. I take a

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.500
little bit of issue with 'strong female characters', as though,

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:36.800
you know, why do you have to put the word strong front

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:41.100
of it? There are strong male characters

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:43.900
and weak male characters. There are strong female characters.

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:46.900
and weak female characters. There are strong and weak

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:50.200
trans characters. You can kind of look at a spectrum and

00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:56.600
on one end of the spectrum is misogyny and neglect. Misogyny,

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:59.200
we all know what that means, but there's a sort of indirect

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:03.100
form of misogyny, which is like the absence of female characters

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:08.700
or  substantial storylines for them, or that they're

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.000
just relegated to their body or just to their relationship

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.500
to a man, right? So that's one end of the spectrum and

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:20.200
that's bad. The other end of the spectrum is trying to

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:25.000
place women on some sort of pedestal, you know, trying

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:34.200
to make them these sort of goddesses or fantasies and 

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:38.500
almost trying too hard to make them strong, you know, 

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:42.500
which is another way. I actually am neglecting the

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:49.200
complexity of their lives. So, if you avoid those two ends

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:53.700
and just say, well, we're just going to say this character,

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:58.200
man or woman has a need. What is that thing that they need

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:03.300
in order willing to do to to get that need? 

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.600
Then you can begin to address gender in a sophisticated

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:11.100
way. Obviously, there are certain things that are different

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:14.800
in the human experience between men and women. If Frank

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:19.000
Underwood were having perimenopausal hot flashes, that

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:22.400
would be strange. Like that's not an experience a guy's going

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:27.600
to have. It is an experience that women can and will have.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:32.300
So, we told that story for Claire in part in season 1.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:37.000
But here's the big thing: that wasn't her story. That

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:41.200
was part of her life. But if we had said, this is her

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:45.400
story and her story is only one that is relegated to something

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:48.600
that's gender-specific, then we would have been reductive

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:51.000
with her. But if we're just telling the story of Claire

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:54.900
Underwood and it so happens because she's this age, that

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:57.000
this is something she's going through, then we're just

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:59.900
being realistic. So, I don't know if that answers your

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:02.900
question, but that's that's how we try to approach it.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:07.800
And then the first part of your question, research.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:12.000
For House of Cards, it's a mixture of book learning

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:15.700
of experts, Jay Carson, the guy I talked about earlier is

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.700
our political consultant. If he doesn't know something,

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:20.800
he puts us in touch with people that are. It's my own personal

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:23.900
experience, and it's also my writers' own personal experience

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:27.200
And oftentimes, the research is not so much political.

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:30.400
It's drawing from, you know, you could see something happen

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:37.500
on its sidewalk, like a man screaming. You know, a homeless

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:42.000
man screaming on a sidewalk, that's research. If you absorb

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:45.900
it, if it means something to you, if you find yourself

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:49.700
using it and colliding it with your character, you know.

00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:52.800
So, I think it's about being open on other subject matter

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:56.900
like Garry Kasparov. I mean saying, same thing,

00:28:56.900 --> 00:29:01.300
I mean, it's really project-specific, but you know,

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:05.900
the adage 'write what you know' is a very wise

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:10.500
one but also it's important to write what you don't know,

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.100
if only to discover the things that you know that you didn't

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:22.200
know. Cheers. Hello, finally, gotten more. I got into NYU

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:26.700
Tisch earlier this year to do a masters in screenwriting.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:30.800
But the same time I got an agent and two of my script got picked

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:34.400
up, and I'm developing one of them now, which is a sitcom,

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:37.500
which is kind of a weird experience. I don't really know what

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:41.500
I'm doing, but I'm just asking you, is there any advice

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:44.900
you can give for the next steps that I'm in? Because it's

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:49.300
all based around uncertainty and I've really no idea apart

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:52.800
from the ideas I have in my head about what I'm creating.

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:58.300
Yeah. Is there any advice? The only real advice I could

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:04.500
give is to do the work. I mean, you know, I always struggle

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:07.500
when writers ask for advice because, you know, there's

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:10.700
no formula. I mean, congrats on getting an agent and congrats

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:14.100
on NYU. It's a great school. And I mean, it sounds like

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:17.000
you're creating the opportunities for yourself

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.300
that are beginning to pay off. And, you know, a big

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:26.100
part of any form of success in any industry is looking

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:28.900
for opportunities. And then making opportunities, if they're

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:32.100
not being laid at your feet, and that can be school. It

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:35.300
could be going out and making your own film on your own.

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:40.500
It can be, you know, if no one is doing any of your pilots,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.200
writing a play and doing that with your friends. No one

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:46.200
can stop you from that. There's a million ways to create

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:49.700
opportunities for yourself. But ultimately, all of that

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:54.900
is only there if you've got the pages. The only thing

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:59.900
you can do that is completely in your control and it ultimately

00:30:59.900 --> 00:31:04.900
will make you successful, at least financially or whatever,

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:09.700
as a form of success, although the real success has nothing

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:13.900
to do with that but it's having the pages and putting in

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:16.900
the time and the thousands of hours and thousands of pages

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:25.900
you must write. I don't think that talking about what you're

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:31.100
writing counts. I don't think thinking about what you

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:34.400
want to write counts. I think the only thing that counts

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:41.100
is the actual writing. So, you know, if you've got a sitcom

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:46.100
that's getting some traction, you know, there's a lot of

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:49.000
strategic things that you know, if we sat down for an hour

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.700
and talked about what this agent is doing this and

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:54.400
going to this studio, there's all that bulls[__], 

00:31:54.700 --> 00:32:00.200
that’s strategerly. But no matter what happens,

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:03.300
you're either going to be making your show in which case

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:07.900
it's not about buying a new car and you know, going into all

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:10.900
the clubs. It's about you've got to put your shoulder to the

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:14.000
wheel even more or you're not going to make your show

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:18.200
in which case, the only thing that you can do is write

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:21.000
the next one and then write the next one. And then write

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:23.700
the next one. And then write the next one. And the next

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.700
one, and the next one, and the next one and never stop

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:32.500
until you die.

00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:50.900
I will die, right? Which sounds morbid, but it's

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:54.300
not to me. It's completely liberating because it can't

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:58.900
get worse from there. Like, you have that thought, and

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:05.800
you go, okay. Let's get to work, you know, so that's a

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:08.900
piece of advice to just remember that every day, when you

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:13.800
wake up, puts things in perspective. That seems like a

00:33:13.800 --> 00:33:16.600
wonderful moment on which to end our evening. Thank you

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.100
so much for coming because all that mattered was the story.

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:22.500
Nothing else, not your ego, not your training, not your

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.400
ambitions. This story is all I care about, you know, we're

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:30.300
in this production office in Dublin, and I went upstairs

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:34.800
and I borrowed somebody's computer. And I wrote the scene

